+`revisionId`, `visitId`, `baseRevisionId`, `payload`, `authorId`, `receivedAt`, and `sequence` never change after storage. Only `status` is mutable, and only by the operations below.
+
+### The single-current invariant
+
+A visit has at most one revision with status `current`. Submissions for one visit are serialized, and the base-revision comparison and the resulting writes are a single atomic step. Two submissions that name the same base revision are decided by that serialization: the first is accepted against the base, the second finds the base no longer current and is stored as competing. No submission is discarded by the race.
+
+## Payload
+
+The payload of a revision is the complete submitted visit:
+
+| Field | Type | Rule |
+|---|---|---|
+| `plotId` | UUID | Must name a plot the site catalog knows, including a plot that has since been archived; equals the visit's `plotId` |
+| `observationDate` | Date | Required; must not be in the future |
+| `observers` | List of names | Required, may be empty; each entry non-empty |
+| `speciesCover` | List of entries | Each entry has a `species` name and a `coverPercent` from 0 to 100; species names are distinct within a visit |
+| `notes` | Text | Optional; free text |
+
+Species names are compared as written. The record store does not normalize, synonymize, or infer species identity — botanical interpretation is outside this node and outside the project's scope. The same species written two ways is therefore two entries, exactly as the worker submitted them.
+
+Plot validation runs against the site's authoritative catalog, not against a snapshot cached on a tablet, so that a validation made days after the visit was collected reflects the catalog as it stands when the submission arrives. The catalog must therefore expose a server-side lookup by plot UUID that reports whether a UUID is known and whether its plot is archived; that operation belongs to the site-catalog node, and this node consumes it. A plot that has been archived is still known and still acceptable in a submission, because a correction to a visit recorded before the plot was archived is legitimate. Only a UUID the catalog does not know causes a rejection.
+
+A submission is validated as a whole. A payload that fails validation is rejected in full, and the rejection names every field that failed. Unknown fields are rejected rather than ignored, at any level of the payload, so that a schema mismatch between tablet and server is visible instead of silently dropping data.
+
+## Submission Contract
+
+A submission envelope carries an idempotency key, the visit ID, the base revision ID, and the complete visit payload. The base revision ID is the revision the submitter was looking at when they made their edit. A submission that creates a visit carries no base revision ID.
+
+The record store authenticates the submitting worker, checks the idempotency key, validates the payload, and then determines the concurrency outcome. Submissions are serialized per visit for the duration of that determination and its writes. These checks apply in that order, so a submission that violates several rules reports the first applicable reason: a replayed key is answered as a replay even if the visit has since been created, and a payload that fails validation is reported as invalid even if its base revision is also unknown.
+
+### Idempotency
+
+An idempotency key is scoped to the authenticated worker. The store remembers the key with a hash of the accepted payload and the revision that was created.
+
+- A repeated key with an identical payload returns the original revision identifier and that revision's present status, and creates nothing. The replay does not create a second revision and does not join a review round a second time.
+- A repeated key with a different payload is rejected, and the original submission is left unchanged.
+
+### Concurrency outcome
+
+**The visit does not exist and the submission carries no base revision ID.** The visit is created with that payload, and the submission becomes the visit's first current revision.
+
+**The visit exists and the submission carries no base revision ID.** The submission is rejected as a duplicate creation of an existing visit.
+
+**The base revision is not one of the visit's revisions.** The submission is rejected. The record store cannot place the revision in the visit's lineage and cannot verify what the submitter was looking at. A base revision from another visit is rejected by the same rule, since revision identifiers are only meaningful within a visit.
+
+**The base revision is the visit's current revision and the visit has no open review round.** The submission is stored and becomes the visit's new current revision. The previous current revision becomes `superseded`. No review work is created.
+
+**The base revision is the visit's current revision and the visit has an open review round.** The submission is stored as `competing` and joins the open review round. The current revision does not change.
+
+**The base revision is one of the visit's other revisions.** The submission is stored as `competing` and joins the visit's open review round, opening one if there is none. The current revision does not change.
+
+The last three cases together are the whole concurrency rule: a visit advances only on a submission that names its current revision, and only while no review work is outstanding. A visit under review does not advance, because any revision submitted against a contested state is itself contested. This holds however small the difference between the two revisions, including when the two submissions touched unrelated fields. The record store does not compare fields to decide whether two revisions can be reconciled, because two whole-visit revisions can conflict in scientifically related ways that field names do not reveal.
+
+A visit has at most one open review round. A submission that arrives while a round is open joins that round; a submission that arrives after the round closed opens a new one.
+
+### Responses
+
+An accepted submission returns the new revision's identifier, its status, and the visit's current revision identifier. The current revision identifier lets a tablet name the correct base for its next edit whether its own submission was accepted as current or as competing. A replayed submission returns the same three elements, with the current revision identifier reflecting the visit's state at the time of the replay.
+
+A rejected submission returns the reason and, where the reason is a specific field, the list of failing fields. Every rejection carries a reason, so a worker is never left guessing why an edit did not land. A rejected submission is not stored as a revision; it is recorded in the rejection log, which retains the author, the visit, the reason, the failing fields, and the time.
+
+## Review Rounds and Resolution
+
+A visit has unresolved review work exactly when it has an open review round. A round opens when the first competing revision is stored and closes only at resolution, so an open round always holds at least one competing revision. While review work is outstanding:
+
+- The current revision remains the visit's state of record and is unchanged.
+- The competing revisions are retained with their authors, receipt times, and payloads.
+- The store reports the visit as having unresolved review work and names the competing revisions, so that review can display them side by side.
+
+Each period of contention is a review round, with its own identity. A round opens when the first competing revision is stored for a visit, gathers every competing revision stored against it, and closes when a coordinator resolves it. A round's membership is fixed once the round closes, so the set of revisions considered by each resolution stays inspectable.
+
+### Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves a visit's review work by naming one revision from the open round. The resolution:
+
+- makes the named revision the visit's current revision;
+- marks the visit's previous current revision and the other revisions of the round `superseded`, retaining them for inspection;
+- closes the round and clears the visit's unresolved review work;
+- records the resolution: the coordinator's identity, the chosen revision, and every revision of the round that was considered, including the chosen one.
+
+Only a revision belonging to the open round can be chosen. Choosing a superseded revision, a revision from an earlier round, or the current revision is rejected. Resolving a visit that has no open round changes nothing, writes no resolution record, and returns the visit's current state.
+
+A revision that a coordinator's choice passed over stays retained and inspectable. A submission that arrives after a resolution naming a revision the resolution superseded is stored as competing and opens a new round: the late edit was made against a state that is no longer the visit's state, so it cannot become current on its own.
+
+A coordinator who believes none of the competing revisions is correct may submit a new revision for the visit, naming the visit's current revision as its base while the round is open. That revision joins the open round as competing and is resolved by the same rule as any other, so the coordinator's own revision requires the same review as a worker's. Whether the store should offer a distinct coordinator-authored resolution — one that becomes current without a second round — is an open question below.
+
+## Retrieval Contract
+
+**Current-state retrieval** returns the visit's identity and current revision in full — identifier, base revision, payload, author, receipt time, sequence — whether unresolved review work exists, and, when it does, the open review round with each competing revision in full, including its identifier and payload. Competing payloads are part of the response because review compares whole visits side by side.
+
+**Submission-history retrieval** returns every revision stored for a visit, ordered by sequence, each with its identifier, base revision, status, sequence, payload, author, and receipt time. Payloads are included so that a superseded revision remains inspectable.
+
+**Export-eligible visit set** returns the visits that have a current revision and no open review round. This is the set the partner CSV is drawn from.
+
+A request naming an unknown visit is refused with a not-found result rather than an empty response. A request lacking the required role is refused before the visit is looked up, so a caller without the coordinator role cannot learn whether a visit exists.
+
+Current-state retrieval is available to an authenticated worker. Submission-history retrieval and resolution require the coordinator role, which the record store reads from the identity the team already uses. The record store does not replace the team's identity provider.
+
+## Export Eligibility
+
+A visit is eligible for the partner CSV when it has a current revision and no open review round. A visit with outstanding review work is held out of the export until its review work is resolved, and becomes eligible again once resolved.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency mechanism | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | The team accepts a larger review queue in exchange for never having merge rules combine scientifically related edits without noticing. A whole-visit revision keeps each submitted visit intact and inspectable, and no rule has to guess whether two edits to the same species are one concern or two. The cost is coordinator work on edits that would have merged cleanly, which two coordinators can absorb. |
+| Basis for advancing a visit | The base revision named by the submission | Server receipt order; last writer wins | Receipt order would make a late-arriving stale edit look authoritative. Lineage through the base revision lets the record store show which revision a submitter was looking at, and flags a disagreement instead of resolving it. |
+| Treatment of a visit under review | Hold further submissions as competing revisions and leave the current revision unchanged | Merge incoming revisions into the open review set; let the newest submission become current while flagged | Merging is the field-aware mechanism this node rejected. Letting the newest submission become current would make a disputed visit's exported state depend on arrival order. |
+| Reconciliation of competing revisions | A coordinator selects one revision from the open round | Automatic selection of the newest revision; automatic selection by field overlap | Neither automatic rule can judge scientific agreement without the field-level rules the team chose not to maintain. A coordinator deciding also leaves an attributable record of the choice. |
+| Response to a base revision the visit does not have | Reject the submission | Store it as competing; store it as current | A revision whose base cannot be located has no place in the visit's lineage, and admitting it would let an unanchored payload become the visit's state. |
+| Conflicts on unrelated fields | Every non-current base produces review work, however small the difference | Detect unrelated-field overlap and accept those revisions | Detecting overlap is field-aware merging by another name, and it requires the rules about which fields relate that the team declined to maintain. |
+| Ordering of a visit's revisions | A per-visit sequence number assigned when the revision is stored | Receipt timestamp alone | Receipt timestamps can tie or move with clock adjustments. A sequence number gives a total order that a comparison screen can rely on, and it reflects the order in which the store committed revisions. |
+| Species names in a payload | Compared as written, no normalization | Canonical species identifiers from the catalog | Resolving botanical identity is outside the project's scope, and a canonical identifier would move taxonomic judgment into the store. |
+| Rejected submissions | Recorded in a rejection log, not stored as revisions | Store every rejected submission as a revision; discard rejected submissions outright | A rejected submission has no place in the visit's lineage. Logging it keeps the promise that no submitted edit disappears without a trace, without letting an unusable payload into the visit's history. |
+| Order of checks on a submission | Authentication, idempotency, payload validation, concurrency outcome | Cheapest-check-first; business rules before validation | A fixed order gives one predictable reason per rejected submission, so a tablet can tell a replay from an invalid payload from a stale base and act accordingly. |
+| Shape of the export-eligible set | A set the record store returns, alongside a per-visit eligibility flag on current state | Per-visit flag only; the exporter assembling the set itself | The exporter needs a set it can page through without fetching every visit, and a coordinator needs the flag on a single visit. The record store owns the rule, so it owns both ways of asking. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. The concurrency mechanism is whole-visit revisions.
+2. Revision lineage is expressed through the base revision ID, not arrival order.
+3. Unresolved review work holds the current revision in place and adds further submissions to the open round.
+4. A visit's payload schema is fixed, validated as a whole, and rejects unknown fields.
+5. A visit's plot reference is fixed at creation.
+6. A visit has at most one open review round; a later stale submission opens a new one.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether coordinators may author a corrected revision as a distinct resolution, separate from selecting one of the submitted revisions.
+2. Whether coordinators annotate a resolution with a reason, beyond the coordinator identity and considered revisions already retained.
+3. How long revisions remain retrievable after a visit's season closes.
+4. The site-catalog node's server-side plot lookup operation. This node's plot validation depends on it (REC-023, REC-052, REC-053), but the operation and its specs belong to the site-catalog segment and are not written here.
+
